;; Gate thresholds and budgets, as data rather than constants in code.
;;
;; This is the AHE component-observability idea applied cheaply: every
;; threshold is a file a run records the digest of, so a later evolution loop
;; has somewhere to write and every pass-rate change localizes to one file.
;;
;; Each budget declares its KIND, which is the distinction dirge PR 739 found
;; made the thresholds legible:
;;
;;   :cost-ceiling  bounds sub-LLM spend. Never scaled up for a struggling run
;;                  — that means spending more on the run already in trouble.
;;   :re-fire-guard compensates for a predicate that cannot tell "happened"
;;                  from "happened and I already reacted". Safe to scale.
;;   :threshold     a real decision boundary.
;;
;; `capability-tunable?` is false wherever the capability tier has no standing
;; to move the number. The tier observes tool-call mechanics only, so it may
;; tune argument-hygiene budgets and may never relax a verification or
;; progress guard (dirge PR 740).

{:cull-threshold
 {:value 3 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Consecutive failed verifications before a branch is culled."}

 :cull-recent-window
 {:value 5 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "A confirmed artifact this recently protects a branch from culling."}

 :stuck-threshold
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Consecutive unproductive verifications before the stuck gate fires.

        STRICTLY BELOW cull-threshold, and that is the whole point (vf-31m).
        Both were 3, so the gate that says change your approach arrived on the
        turn the branch became eligible to be killed for not having changed
        it — and every move it predicts (retract, decompose, change technique)
        costs at least one turn. The README recorded the consequence without
        naming the cause: stuck fired and was obeyed zero times. A gate whose
        advice takes turns to follow, firing at the moment turns run out, is
        broken on inspection.

        gate-config-is-coherent asserts the inequality, so a later tuning pass
        cannot quietly close the gap again."}

 :reframe-grace
 {:value 4 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns a branch is spared the consecutive-failure cull after the stuck
        gate has dropped it into a reframe, and the window the withheld
        approach stays withheld for.

        A branch told to abandon its approach carries the failures that caused
        the reframe, so without this it is culled mid-reframe for exactly the
        thing it was told to stop doing (vf-31m). The reprieve machinery
        already existed for Pareto-spared branches and is documented there as
        'a loan with a clock, not an exemption'; this is the same shape.

        The loan and the restriction expire together, deliberately: it would
        be incoherent to keep refusing an approach after the branch has lost
        the protection it was given to replace it. cull-hard-multiple still
        ends it early — a branch failing at twice the cull threshold is not
        reframing."}

 :progress-stall-threshold
 {:value 4 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns with no progress event before the stall nudge fires. Arms only
        after the first progress event; see prologue-cap for the other case."}

 :prologue-cap
 {:value 8 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns a branch may take having produced nothing at all. The stall
        counter arms on a progress event, so a run that produced nothing never
        armed it and the monitor was structurally blind to the one case it most
        needed to report (dirge PR 739, dirge-t5dh)."}

 :max-stall-nudges
 {:value 2 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How often the stall nudge may re-fire on one branch."}

 :studying-turns
 {:value 10 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Consecutive inspection-only turns (read/grep/eval/…), after the branch
        has shipped at least once, before the supervisor's studying gate fires.
        High enough that opening exploration is never nagged; a live run
        over-studied for twenty (karamazov-j5t)."}

 :max-studying-nudges
 {:value 3 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How often the studying nudge may re-fire on one branch."}

 :max-reflection-nudges
 {:value 3 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often the reflection nudge may re-fire on one branch.

        It had no budget at all — it fires on a cadence rather than on a
        condition, so nothing bounded it. Measured across four live runs: 11
        firings, zero compliance. An unbounded nudge that nothing ever acts on
        is a per-firing tax on the branch's context, paid to ask again for
        something already declined ten times.

        Not capability-tunable, for the same reason the cadence is not: the
        tier under observation should not loosen the terms on which it is
        invited to reshape the machinery observing it.

        Three rather than one because the ask is legitimate — a run that never
        reconsiders its own machinery keeps whatever friction it started with
        — and the branch may only be in a position to act on it later in a
        run. If the met-rate stays at zero with a budget, the ask itself is
        aimed at the wrong role, and that is the next thing to change."}

 :max-stuck-hints
 {:value 3 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How often the stuck hint may re-fire on one branch."}

 :max-milestone-nudges
 {:value 1 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The milestone fires once per branch, on the first confirmation."}

 :max-emergency-reviews
 {:value 1 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The emergency review fires once per branch. It was the one steer gate
        with no guard, and a live knights-3 run (2026-08-06) showed why that
        matters: its precondition held across three consecutive boundaries and
        it re-fired on every one, all predictions unmet — the exact
        happened-versus-already-reacted blindness re-fire guards exist for.
        Once: the steer either lands or the cull proceeds on its own."}

 :max-done-blocks
 {:value 3 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often `done` may be refused before the branch is told to give up.
        A cost ceiling because each refusal spends another model turn."}

 :turn-budget-notices
 {:value [0.6 0.85] :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Fractions of the turn cap at which the model is told where it stands.
        The cap was enforced but invisible to the model (dirge PR 738)."}

 :wind-down-fraction
 {:value 0.85 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Fraction of the turn cap at which the branch is told to stop exploring
        and ship what it has. Sized so the reserved tail fits an audit plus a
        done call for a branch with confirmed artifacts: UCLA's harness found a
        run with no reserved shipping tail spends its whole budget exploring
        and ships nothing."}

 :max-wind-down-steers
 {:value 1 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often the wind-down rung may fire on one branch. Once: the steer
        either lands or it does not."}

 :last-call-window
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How many of a branch's final turns the last-call rung fires in. The
        wind-down rung asks a branch to ship and is a soft nudge a model can
        ignore — a live team run (karamazov-pab) had every worker burn its whole
        cap without one done or give_up. This rung fires in the last turns and
        FORCES a done attempt (a done-tool prefill, not prose), so a run that
        will not converge at least ships its best partial rather than exhausting
        silently. Small: it is the last resort, not a second wind-down."}

 :max-last-call-steers
 {:value 2 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How often the last-call rung may fire on one branch. Matched to the
        window so it can force a done on each of the final turns."}

 :max-safe-state-aborts
 {:value 2 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often the safe-state rung may fire on one branch. Not
        capability-tunable: this rung spends a hard-capped abort and, in a
        non-advisory mode, rebuilds an engine process."}

 :max-total-branches
 {:value 8 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Total branches per run including forked children. A cost ceiling:
        every branch is a concurrent provider call per turn plus its own
        engine process.

        Lowered from 15 after gen-21 measured what 15 actually costs. Asked
        for width 5, it reached 11 active by round 18 — beam_width is the
        floor the harness refills toward, not a cap — and spent 2.46M tokens
        for 4 confirmed artifacts before being stopped. The forks were
        genuinely distinct, so this is not a diversity problem; it is that
        the ceiling was set without anyone having measured the bill."}

 :critic-every
 {:value 5 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns between critic scorings of one branch. Every scoring is a
        sub-LLM call, so this is spend, and it is per active branch."}

 :cull-hard-multiple
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Multiple of cull-threshold at which a Pareto-spared branch is culled
        regardless of its scores. The reprieve is a loan with a clock, not an
        exemption — Pareto's known weakness is permissiveness."}

 :fork-invite-floor
 {:value {:viability 4 :progress 3} :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Minimum critic scores before a branch is invited to fork. Below
        this, growth spends budget on lines not yet earning it."}

 :juvenile-grace
 {:value 8 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns a newly forked branch may take before the cull rule applies to
        it at all. Generation 9 forked for the first time and every child was
        dead within twelve turns: progress and momentum are age-correlated,
        so a branch born at turn 15 scores 1 on progress by definition and its
        mature parent dominates it on every objective one turn later.
        Selection that runs before an offspring can express itself culls on
        age rather than on merit. Sized to fit a thesis, a first encoding and
        a first verification — long enough to produce one confirmation, short
        enough that a genuinely dead line does not linger."}

 :branch-out-cooldown
 {:value 6 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Turns before the branch-out rung may ask the same branch again.
        Generation 9 fired it on turns 14, 15 and 16 — one fork, then two
        re-fires while the branch was still acting on the first — and the
        whole fork budget was gone by turn 16 of a 65-turn run. The
        precondition (a confirmation exists, the beam has room) stays true
        for as long as the branch is productive, which is exactly the
        happened-versus-already-reacted blindness a cooldown fixes."}

 :max-branch-outs
 {:value 6 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often the branch-out rung may fire on one branch. This is the
        reproduction half of the loop, so it is a guard rather than a cost
        ceiling: each firing asks for offspring, and a branch that keeps
        confirming should keep being asked, but not on every boundary while
        it is busy complying."}

 :max-repopulates
 {:value 8 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often the repopulate rung may ask one branch to refill the beam.
        Higher than :max-branch-outs, because this fires when the beam is
        already below width and a run that stops asking at that point simply
        finishes at width one — which is what five straight campaign runs did.
        The per-branch fork-invite cooldown still spaces the asks out, so this
        is the ceiling on a long collapse, not on a burst."}

 :fork-invite-cooldown
 {:value 6 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Turns before the same branch may be invited to fork again. The
        invitation predicate cannot tell 'strong' from 'strong and already
        asked', which is the shape re-fire guards exist for."}

 :explore-cap
 {:value 5 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns a branch may spend in the explore prologue before it is forced
        into build. The sketch tool is new — no generation has run with it —
        so 5 is a judgement call with no data behind it: a couple of searches
        and a sketch attempt or two, against prologue-cap's 8. The forced
        transition is the release valve that keeps a branch which cannot get
        a skeleton to elaborate from being locked out of verification for the
        whole run."}

 :cull-mechanics-multiple
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Multiple of cull-threshold at which consecutive mechanics failures
        (malformed tool calls and harness refusals) cull a branch even with
        survivors to spare. Three bad fences is a model having a bad turn;
        twice that is a branch that cannot work the protocol."}

 :safe-state-multiple
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Multiple of cull-threshold in consecutive failures before the
        safe-state rung may fire. Deliberately harder to trigger than a
        cull: this rung spends a hard-capped abort and, in a non-advisory
        mode, rebuilds an engine process."}

 ;; --- the storm guard (karamazov-ekk, after dirge storm.rs) ---------------
 ;; Detection is samizdat.agent.storm (pure fns over a per-branch window of
 ;; (tool, canonical-args) signatures); the withhold itself is the phases.edn
 ;; :storm / :storm-oscillation refusal rules; the words are
 ;; resources/prompts/storm*.md. Everything tunable is HERE, because dirge
 ;; hardcodes its 6/3 window and cannot tune it at runtime — we can.

 :storm-enabled
 {:value true :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Whether the storm guard withholds calls at all. The off switch is
        policy, not a rebuild: a project whose workflow legitimately repeats
        identical calls (polling a build, say) can turn the guard off or
        widen the threshold instead of fighting it."}

 :storm-window-size
 {:value 6 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How many recent dispatched calls the storm window remembers per
        branch. Exempt (read-only) tools never enter; a mutating call clears
        the non-mutating entries, so a check-fix-check cycle does not
        accumulate."}

 :storm-threshold
 {:value 3 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "The Nth identical (tool, canonical-args) call is withheld: at 3 the
        first two run — one call plus one honest retry — and the third is
        refused with the reflect-then-pivot prompt. Must be at least 2. The
        guard withholds rather than warns: the begin-reframe experience is
        that gates which merely suggest went 0-for-4, and dirge found the
        bare don't-repeat-yourself nudge reinforces the failing chain of
        thought (degeneration-of-thought), which is why the message demands
        named assumptions and fundamentally different alternatives instead."}

 :storm-timeout-floor
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "The effective threshold for a signature that has TIMED OUT: a hung
        call burned its whole time budget, so it does not get the full retry
        allowance — the second identical copy is withheld, not the third."}

 :storm-verify-exempt
 {:value true :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Whether a shell call running the project's :run :verify-cmd is
        invisible to the storm guard. The verify loop IS repetition — red,
        edit, rerun the identical suite command — and the best run of the
        GLM campaign (4e785664) contained exactly one identical repeat: its
        verify command. Blocking the third suite run would punish TDD
        itself. Rerunning a red suite with NO edit between stays the
        failure-keyed guards' business: repeating-failure? sees the
        identical error, the stuck gate sees the streak."}

 :storm-min-cycles
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Full A-B-A-B cycles between exactly two signatures before the call
        that would extend the alternation is withheld. Exactly two distinct
        signatures, deliberately: near-alternation among three or more calls
        is divergence, and counting it manufactures phantom oscillation."}

 :storm-strikes-to-force
 {:value 3 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Consecutive storm-withheld calls before the :storm gate forces an
        honest give_up. Withholding blocks the repeat; this rung answers the
        model that keeps asking for it anyway — the run is spending turns
        re-requesting a result it already has, and an honest give_up hands
        the task back where a silent grind hands back nothing. Any
        dispatched (non-withheld) tracked call resets the count."}

 :max-storm-forces
 {:value 2 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How often the :storm gate may force a give_up on one branch."}

 :storm-error-digest-chars
 {:value 160 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How much of a failed call's error text the storm window remembers per
        entry, quoted back when the same call is retried (retry-diagnosis).
        A digest, not the whole error: the window lives on the branch and
        rides every checkpoint."}

 :file-thrash-threshold
 {:value 8 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Consecutive file-touching calls whose path sets overlap before the
        file-thrash gate reminds the branch what it set out to do (dirge
        context_depth.rs, its recommended value). Counts calls, not turns;
        the tracked set narrows to the intersection so divergent touches
        break the streak on their own; a no-file call resets it."}

 :max-file-thrash-nudges
 {:value 1 :kind :re-fire-guard :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How often the file-thrash gate may fire on one branch. One-shot per
        run by default: it is a reminder, and a reminder repeated is a nag
        the settle table will record being ignored."}

 :timeout-failure-weight
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "What a timed-out call adds to consecutive-failures, where an ordinary
        failure adds 1. A timeout is the most expensive failure there is —
        it burned a whole time budget — and counting it like a millisecond
        error made a branch stuck retrying a hang the weakest possible
        signal, one cheap-looking failure per 120s. The streak gates
        (:stuck, :safe-state) read the counter unchanged and so trip sooner
        on expensive loops."}

 :trajectory-score
 {:value
  {:repeats 4
   :stride 5
   :abandon-below 0.05
   :criteria
   ["concrete-output: look only at files written or edited and commands run.
     HIGH: new or corrected code landing on disk that serves the goal. LOW:
     steps that read, list, or plan without producing anything on disk.
     Ignore the agent's own narration."
    "verification-signal: look only at test and verify command results. HIGH:
     the suite or a focused test moving from red toward green. LOW: repeated
     red with the same error, or no verification at all. Ignore claims of
     success not backed by an actual run."
    "convergence: look at the sequence of recent steps. HIGH: each step
     builds on the previous step's result. LOW: repeats of the same call,
     alternation between two calls, or unconnected jumps. Ignore verbosity."]}
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "Continuous trajectory scoring (karamazov-fut, llm-as-a-verifier's
        ProgressTracker): every :stride-th step judged on steps-so-far,
        :repeats letter-scale asks averaged, criteria per the verifier
        TEMPLATE rules (2-4 narrow ones beating one broad one; each says
        where to look, what is HIGH, what is LOW, what to ignore).
        :abandon-below is the eventual flat-low-curve decision threshold —
        shipped as data from day one, CONSULTED BY NOTHING until the signal
        is validated on baseline runs (samizdat.agent.trajectory/score-run):
        a completed and an exhausted run must actually separate before any
        gate or cull reads the number."}

 :max-branch-theses
 {:value 4 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Most theses one branch_theses call may propose. A cost ceiling:
        every proposal beyond the first becomes a sibling branch — a
        concurrent provider call per turn plus its own process."}

 :reflection-cadence
 {:value 15 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Turns between reflection nudges. Not capability-tunable: the nudge
        invites the branch to reshape its own loop (reload_cells), and the
        tier under observation should not loosen the cadence at which it is
        invited to mutate the machinery observing it."}

 :share
 {:value {:statuses #{:confirmed} :require-relevance? true}
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The shared-pool entry condition (drg-4026 #5): which claim statuses
        an artifact must carry to export to sibling branches, and whether the
        relevance filter (advances-thesis?) additionally gates it. Engine-
        confirmed is the entry condition that separates this from
        self-reported results; relevance is the second one — an artifact
        that engages neither the branch's thesis nor the problem cost its
        own branch a turn, exported it becomes every branch's context
        (vf-8fl). require-relevance? off admits any confirmed artifact."}

 :fork-inherit
 {:value {:inherit? true :depth nil}
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "What a forked child inherits from its parent (LR-1).

        :inherit? — whether the child opens on its parent's CONVERSATION or
        on a fresh [system, problem] tape. Fresh was the old behaviour and it
        was not a decision anyone made: a fork discarded everything its
        parent had learned and re-derived it from the problem statement,
        while the crossover block's own prose claimed the child carried its
        parent's history. Inheriting is nearly free — the tape is an
        immutable value, so a child shares structure with its parent — and on
        a local endpoint the shared prefix is also a warm KV cache
        (:cache_prompt, LR-5).

        :depth — how many MESSAGES of the parent to carry, nil for all of
        them. Two per exchange, so 8 is four exchanges. This is the
        branch-an-older-turn knob: when a parent went wrong at turn 6, a
        child forked at depth 10 retries from before the mistake instead of
        from zero. Only the gate counters are reset either way — a child is
        never culled for its parent's failures.

        A depth past the parent's own length cannot lengthen the tape, so a
        stale number degrades to a full inherit rather than an error."}

 :probe
 {:value {:width 3 :on-mechanics-failures 2 :variants []}
  :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The non-committing probe's budget (LR-2, LR-3).

        :width — how many candidate framings :probe/next-move tries against
        the branch's current tape before the real turn. Each is one
        completion, so this is spend: 0 turns the probe off entirely. On a
        local endpoint every bounce reuses the same warm prefix
        (:cache_prompt), so a scan costs N completions and ONE prefill;
        against a metered provider it costs N calls, which is why the default
        is small.

        :on-mechanics-failures — how many consecutive malformed or unusable
        calls a branch must have produced before probing is worth it. A branch
        emitting well-formed calls needs no help, and probing it is pure
        spend. This is a :cost-ceiling, so it is never scaled UP for a
        struggling run — a branch in trouble is the last one to spend more on.

        :variants — the role keys :probe/ab-model fans one question across,
        resolved through config :run :role-models. Empty turns the A/B off.
        Recording the comparison is the point; nothing routes on it yet.

        NOT capability-tunable, even though both knobs are keyed on tool-call
        mechanics and the tier's remit is exactly that. It is a cost ceiling,
        and the tier could only ever want to raise :width — which is spending
        MORE on the branch already in trouble, the one thing the cost-ceiling
        kind exists to forbid (dirge PR 740). gate-config-is-coherent asserts
        it, and caught this entry claiming otherwise."}

 :turn-deadline-ms
 {:value 900000 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "A hard ceiling on ONE branch turn, independent of the HTTP layer's own
        timeout.

        Found by the first live beam: B1's provider call hung, and because the
        scheduler is a barrier the whole beam sat still behind it for fifteen
        minutes. The socket timeout does not always fire — the connection sits
        ESTABLISHED with zero throughput and the caller waits forever. This is
        the RAX-manager principle applied to a branch: the stop path must not
        depend on the component's cooperation.

        Sized to the worst LEGITIMATE turn rather than the typical one, because
        forfeiting a turn that was about to succeed is expensive twice over —
        the branch loses the turn AND the failure is logged for other branches
        to avoid. The worst legitimate turn is a provider call at its 300000ms
        socket timeout followed by an engine call at its own 300000ms, so the
        old 420000ms could not fit one without a false kill.

        A project whose checks are slower should raise it, which is exactly why
        it is here rather than a constant in code. Skipped entirely for a
        non-iterating manifest (team, feature, decompose), where one 'turn' is
        the branch's whole job.

        HARNESS_TURN_DEADLINE_MS still overrides, for an operator who needs it
        for one run without editing the project's policy."}

 :crossover
 {:value {:statuses #{"confirmed"} :limit 8}
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "What a newly forked child is shown of what OTHER lineages have proved.

        This is the recombination half of the beam: a child that inherits only
        its parent's history deepens one line, while a child opened holding
        what its aunts and uncles confirmed can COMBINE lineages. :statuses is
        which claim statuses qualify — engine-confirmed only, by default, since
        the whole value is that the child can build on it without re-checking.
        :limit bounds how many, newest first, because this lands in the child's
        opening context and an unbounded block would crowd out its own thesis."}

 :verify-timeout-ms
 {:value 600000 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Wall-clock bound on one verification run — the project's own test
        command. Was a 600000 default inside verify.clj, which is the wrong
        place for it twice over: it is the single most project-specific number
        in the harness (a suite that takes twelve minutes and one that takes
        four seconds are both normal), and a branch whose verification is
        guillotined reads as a branch that cannot get its tests green.

        Never scaled UP for a struggling run: a run in trouble spending longer
        per check is how a turn budget disappears into one command."}

 :review-blocking-severities
 {:value ["critical" "high"] :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The finding severities a review BLOCKS a ship on. Anything else is
        advisory — passed back to the branch, not grounds for undoing a done.

        Was a regex literal in judge.clj, which encoded two project judgements
        as code: that a reviewer says [critical]/[high] rather than [P0]/[P1] or
        [blocker], and that medium never blocks. Both are the project's call. An
        empty list makes every finding advisory, which is a legitimate stance
        for a codebase where the reviewer is a second opinion rather than a
        gate."}

 :planner-max-parts
 {:value 4 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How many parts the planner may split a task into — the team fan-out
        width. Kept small because parallel workers share ONE workspace, and a
        wide split multiplies collision risk faster than it buys speed. That is
        a judgement about a project's workspace: a repo of independent packages
        tolerates a much wider split than one with a single build. Config
        :run :max-subtasks still overrides for one run."}

 :board-max-tasks
 {:value 40 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How many tasks one board run will work before it stops and reports what
        is left. The board loop is open-ended by design — an owner may split its
        task into more tasks, and those may split again — so the only structural
        bound is this one. It is a RUNAWAY GUARD, not a plan: a run that reaches
        it has produced 40 owned, reviewed changes and should be looked at, not
        quietly continued. Raise it for a genuinely large migration."}

 :board-review-attempts
 {:value 2 :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How many times a critic may send one task back to its owner before the
        loop leaves it open and moves on. A calibrated critic blocks rarely;
        this caps what a miscalibrated one can spend on a single task. The task
        is NOT closed when this trips — it goes back to the board, because a
        task the board still shows is a truer record than one closed because the
        loop got tired of it."}

 :retention
 {:value {:events-hours 24
          :run-record-days nil}
  :kind :policy :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "HOW LONG THE DATABASE KEEPS A FINISHED RUN. One project, one file, and
        it only ever grew.

        :events-hours — the tail buffer. Events are not a record: every kind
        that matters is also written to a durable table, and their only readers
        are the live tail and last-progress-at. The window exists because a
        client tailing a run reads its :run-finished entry to learn the run
        ended, so events have to outlive the run itself by long enough for
        that. This was (* 24 3600) inline in store/runs.clj.

        :run-record-days — the RECORD: turns, artifacts, failures, gate
        firings and the messages between branches. nil means keep forever, and
        that is the default, deliberately.

        Deleting these is not the same kind of act as deleting events, and the
        difference is the whole reason one defaults on and the other defaults
        off. RFC-009's central property is that a resume rebuilds branch state
        by REPLAY — everything a gate reads is journalled — and that a crashed
        run stays inspectable. Pruning turns ends both for that run: it becomes
        a row saying something happened, with no account of what.

        So this is an operator's decision about disk, not a default anyone
        should inherit. When it is set, only runs that are finished AND older
        than the window are touched, the `runs` row itself always survives (an
        index of what happened, without the bulk), and the FTS mirrors go with
        their rows — an orphaned index entry would keep ranking against
        nothing, which is worse than either keeping or deleting cleanly."}

 :corroboration
 {:value {:min-runs 2}
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "HOW MANY DISTINCT RUNS A PATTERN NEEDS BEFORE IT JUSTIFIES CHANGING THE
        LOOP.

        One run is an observation, not a pattern. A single run can go wrong for
        reasons that have nothing to do with the harness — a flaky provider, an
        unlucky task, a model having a bad day — and a supervisor that retunes
        on one run's evidence is fitting the noise. It will then measure the
        retune against the next run, which is also one run, and so on.

        Adopted from backpass (VISION.md): `a new instruction needs
        corroboration from at least two distinct sessions, and one session
        never counts twice however often it is re-analyzed`. The DISTINCT is
        the load-bearing word — a second sighting inside the same run is the
        same evidence counted twice.

        Two is deliberately the lowest bar that is still a bar. It is not a
        claim that two runs prove anything; it is the difference between a
        pattern and an afternoon, and raising it costs the supervisor its
        ability to react at all on a short campaign.

        Below the bar a finding is still SHOWN — it may be the only warning
        anyone gets — and it is marked as uncorroborated, so acting on it is a
        deliberate choice rather than a mistake."}

 :fitness
 {:value
  {:weights {:tool-success     1.0
             :tool-failure    -1.0
             :tool-mechanics  -1.5
             :parse-error     -2.0
             :no-fence        -2.0
             :truncated       -1.0
             :auto-repaired   -0.25
             :verify-green     3.0
             :verify-red      -1.0
             :verify-skipped  -2.0
             :gate-met         0.5
             :gate-met-late    0.25
             :gate-unmet      -0.5
             :provider-empty  -2.0
             :provider-failed -1.5
             :provider-retry  -0.5}
   :min-turns-for-verdict 6
   :meaningful-delta 0.15
   :max-open-experiments 1}
  :kind :policy :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "THE SELECTION PRESSURE. One number per turn saying how well the loop is
        running, so that a change to the loop can be judged against something
        other than a feeling.

        The supervisor mutates the loop — a cell, a manifest, a prompt, a
        threshold — and userspace keeps every version. That is variation and
        heredity. Without a fitness function there is no SELECTION: nothing
        distinguishes a change that helped from one that was merely made, and a
        supervisor accumulates edits the way a codebase accumulates workarounds.

        The weights are the value judgements, and they are here rather than in
        code because they are exactly the thing a project should argue with.
        The ones worth defending:

        :tool-mechanics is worse than :tool-failure. A failed call TESTED
        something and came back negative; a malformed one produced no evidence
        at all and cost the same turn. A loop that cannot tell those apart will
        optimise toward making no claims.

        :parse-error and :no-fence are the heaviest negatives. A turn that
        never reached a tool is a turn spent entirely on the harness's own
        overhead, and it is the failure mode most within the harness's power to
        fix.

        :verify-skipped is worse than :verify-red. Red means the gate ran and
        caught something — that is the gate WORKING. Skipped means it was asked
        and could not answer, and the work shipped anyway.

        :truncated is negative but light, and separate from the parse errors it
        resembles: the repair is more tokens, not more steering, so it should
        push on a different lever.

        :provider-empty is as heavy as a parse error and for the same reason —
        a reply with neither content nor reasoning is a turn spent entirely on
        overhead. It is separate from :provider-failed because the responses
        differ: an empty reply means the model spent its budget thinking and
        wants more tokens or reasoning off, while a failed call wants waiting.
        A retry that eventually succeeded still costs, so it counts, lightly.

        Per TURN, not per run, so runs of different lengths compare.

        :max-open-experiments — how many measured-but-unsettled changes may be
        in flight. One, because two changes measured over the same interval
        tell you nothing about either, and this is ENFORCED rather than asked:
        the prompt has always said one change per round, and a rule the model
        can decline is not a rule. Settling a verdict (revert or keep) frees
        the slot.

        :min-turns-for-verdict — below this, a delta is noise and the honest
        verdict is `too early`. :meaningful-delta — how much per-turn fitness
        must move before a change counts as having done anything, rather than
        the supervisor reading its own preferences into the third decimal."}

 :watch
 {:value {:enabled? true
          :poll-ms 4000
          :severities ["high"]
          :max-interventions 3}
  :kind :policy :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "THE WATCHER: a thread that observes a run while it runs and speaks when
        something is going wrong, the way a person watching a coding harness
        does.

        The supervisor ROLE is a manifest node, so it runs between rounds, in
        sequence, and only in the workflows that wire it. A run losing every
        turn to empty provider replies reaches no round boundary quickly, and
        the node never gets a look. This is the other half: continuous
        observation, reacting while the pattern is still forming.

        It intervenes through the same interventions queue a human uses, so a
        directive still lands on a turn boundary and never touches a branch
        mid-turn. `issued-by` is `watch` instead of `human`; nothing else
        differs.

        :severities — only these are worth interrupting for. Most findings are
        worth SEEING and only some are worth a turn: a supervisor reading a
        block can weigh a medium finding, while a branch mid-task handed one is
        just distracted.

        :max-interventions — per run. Every directive costs the branch a turn
        reading it, so an observer with no budget is an observer that can spend
        the whole run explaining why the run is going badly. Three, and a
        finding is raised at most once either way.

        :poll-ms — how often it looks. Fast enough to catch a pattern while it
        is forming, slow enough that it is not re-deciding on every turn.

        :enabled? false turns it off entirely, which is the right setting for a
        run whose whole purpose is to reproduce a failure."}

 :session-findings
 {:value {:mechanics-rate 0.25
          :repair-rate 0.2
          :truncation-rate 0.15
          :verify-skip-rate 0.5
          :tool-failure-rate 0.5
          :min-tool-calls 4
          :min-gate-firings 3
          :min-total-gate-firings 5
          :gate-met-rate 0.2
          :min-green-runs 2
          :min-culled-with-evidence 2
          :provider-trouble-rate 0.2
          :details
          {:calls-not-parsing "a large share of turns produce no usable tool call"
           :calls-need-repair "the harness is repairing the model's tool calls often — the calls work, but the model is not writing them cleanly"
           :replies-truncated "replies are hitting the token cap before emitting a call. The fix is MORE TOKENS, not more steering: the model never got far enough to call anything"
           :shipping-unverified "the ship gate is being asked and cannot answer — work is shipping without its tests having been run"
           :verification-working "the ship gate is running the tests and they are passing"
           :tool-failing "`{{tool}}` fails more often than it works"
           :gate-ignored "the `{{gate}}` gate has fired {{fired}} times and nothing it asked for has happened"
           :steering-ignored "the branch is not responding to steering AT ALL — {{fired}} gate firings across the run and {{met}} of them got what they asked for. More nagging will not help; the loop itself, or the model, is the thing to change"
           :provider-empty-replies "the provider keeps returning replies with neither content nor reasoning — the model is spending its whole output budget thinking and never reaching a tool call. The fix is MORE TOKENS or reasoning turned off for this endpoint, not more steering: raise :context-budget, or set :thinking? false if this is a local llama.cpp endpoint"
           :provider-unreliable "provider calls are failing or needing retries often. This is the endpoint, not the loop — no prompt or manifest change will help, and turns spent here are turns the run does not get back"
           :selection-losing-evidence "branches are being culled while holding CONFIRMED artifacts — the beam found evidence and the retention rule threw it away. This is a SHARPENING failure, not an expansion one: widening the beam will not help, and the fix is the critic rubric or the cull thresholds"}}
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "WHEN A LIVE SESSION TALLY BECOMES SOMETHING THE SUPERVISOR SHOULD SEE.

        samizdat.session counts what happens as it happens — turns, tools by
        outcome, fences that did not parse, gates that fired and whether what
        they asked for happened, whether the ship gate could run. These are
        the thresholds at which a count stops being noise and becomes a
        finding.

        They are thresholds and not judgement, deliberately. What to DO about
        a quarter of turns producing no usable call is the supervisor's call
        and depends on the run; noticing that it is happening should not be.
        A supervisor that has to measure before it can think will mostly not
        bother, which is how a harness ends up guessing where its problems
        are.

        :mechanics-rate    share of turns yielding no usable tool call
        :repair-rate       share the harness had to repair to use
        :truncation-rate   share cut off by the token cap. Its own signal
                           because the fix is more tokens, not more steering —
                           reading it as a model too weak to call a tool would
                           be the wrong repair entirely.
        :verify-skip-rate  share of ship attempts where the gate was asked and
                           could not answer
        :tool-failure-rate a single tool failing more often than it works
        :min-tool-calls    before a tool's rate means anything
        :min-gate-firings  before a gate's silence means anything
        :min-green-runs    before `verification is working` is worth saying

        The last one is not symmetry for its own sake: a supervisor shown only
        what is broken will keep changing things that are working."}

 :verify-unknown
 {:value :trust
  :kind :policy :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "WHAT TO DO WHEN THE SHIP GATE CANNOT TELL. `:trust` ships, `:refuse`
        blocks and says why.

        The case: verification is configured, but the tests could not be run —
        git could not say what changed, or nothing among the changed files was
        a test namespace and no :verify-cmd was set. The gate has no evidence
        either way.

        `:trust` was a hardcoded fallthrough, on the reasoning that refusing
        would deadlock a loop whose git happened to fail. That reasoning is
        sound and it is also exactly how a misconfiguration became a false
        green: a live run shipped {:test 19 :pass 49 :error 5} because the
        baseline was never captured, the rung was silently inert, and the
        gate trusted.

        Which way this should default is a JUDGEMENT ABOUT THIS PROJECT, not
        a fact about harnesses, and it is the supervisor's to make. In a
        project where git is reliable and the tests are the point, :refuse
        turns a silent false green into a loud complaint. In one where the
        verify command is flaky or the tree is not a git checkout, :trust
        keeps the loop moving. The supervisor has the evidence to tell those
        apart — how often runs shipped and were wrong, whether the gate ever
        actually ran — and `remember` to carry the finding to the next run.

        Whichever it is, the decision is now RECORDED: the ship-verify note
        fires with :ran false and a reason whether or not the tests ran, so a
        run that shipped unverified is no longer indistinguishable in the
        record from one that shipped green."}

 :tool-clip
 {:value
  {:message "\n… [{{shown}} of {{total}} chars shown — the rest was clipped. Ask for the part you still need (a narrower command, a `read_file` with an `offset`, or a filter), not the same call again.]"}
  :kind :policy :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "WHAT A CLIPPED TOOL RESULT SAYS AT ITS CUT.

        It used to say `… [truncated]` and stop. That names a problem and no
        way out of it, and a model that cannot see a way out repeats the call.
        Live, against a 7KB file: six reads through four different tools, the
        same first 4014 characters every time, then four more turns spent
        writing a chunked reader by hand — ten turns of forty.

        So the line carries the two numbers that let a reader decide what to
        do (how much it got, how much exists) and says plainly that repeating
        the call is not the move. `read_file` does better than this and pages
        properly; this is the floor for every other tool, where the harness
        cannot know what a narrower request would look like."}

 :workflow-selection
 {:value
  {:enabled?          true
   :candidates        #{"loop" "decompose" "team" "critic"}
   :fallback          "loop"
   :min-problem-chars 120}
  :kind :policy :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "WHICH WORKFLOW DRIVES A RUN THAT DID NOT ASK FOR ONE.

        `workflow/catalog` was written as the menu the self-healing loop
        chooses from, and nothing chose from it — every caller was in test/. A
        run read :run :loop once from config and nothing ever wrote it, so
        `decompose`, whose whole name is decompose-on-stuck, could only be
        picked BEFORE anything was stuck. Four consecutive live runs of a task
        with obvious separable parts exhausted the factory loop's budget with
        both alternatives sitting unreachable in the catalogue.

        :candidates is a WHITELIST, not the whole catalogue. Several manifests
        say in their own descriptions that they are components — `worker`,
        `reviewer` and `supervisor` are pieces of the feature loop — and a menu
        offering those invites a run driven by half a workflow. `beam`,
        `probe`, `review`, `orchestrator` and `feature` are left off for now
        not because they are wrong but because nothing has yet measured them
        against the four that are on: widening this list is a claim about
        which workflow suits which task, and it should be made on evidence.

        :fallback is what a run uses when selection is off, the call fails, or
        the reply names nothing on the menu. It is the factory loop — what the
        run would have used anyway — so a failure to choose costs nothing.

        An explicit :run :loop (HARNESS_LOOP, or a project's config.edn) always
        wins over selection. This fills the gap where a caller said nothing; it
        never overrides one who did.

        :min-problem-chars is the floor below which no call is made. A one-line
        problem is the factory loop's case by definition — nothing to split,
        nothing to decompose — and being told so is not worth a round trip. At
        0 every run is chosen for.

        Not capability-tunable: the tier under observation should not widen
        the set of machinery it may be driven by."}

 :project-facts
 {:value
  {:cmd-works   "In this project `{{cmd}}` works."
   :cmd-refused "In this project `{{cmd}}` is refused by the shell policy — do not spend a turn on it."
   :workflow    "The `{{cmd}}` workflow has driven a run on this project."}
  :kind :policy :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "THE WORDS A DERIVED PROJECT MEMORY IS WRITTEN IN.

        These two lines are read by the model on every later run, so they are
        prose the model sees and they belong here rather than compiled into
        the store. `{{cmd}}` is the only substitution.

        They are phrased as standing facts about the project rather than as
        reports of an episode — \"works\", not \"worked once\" — because that is
        what they are: a command that ran clean here is a property of this
        checkout, and the next run should read it as one. The refusal line
        carries its own instruction because a refusal the reader does not
        recognise as final is a refusal they will spend a turn re-testing.

        :workflow is the row that carries a workflow's record on this project.
        Its own text barely matters — what a reader acts on is the success and
        failure counts behind it, which samizdat.agent.select renders as
        `shipped N of M runs`. It is here rather than compiled for the same
        reason as the other two: it is a sentence the model reads."}

 :reflection
 {:value
  {:args-chars   160
   :result-chars 240}
  :kind :policy :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "HOW MUCH OF A TURN THE REFLECTOR SEES, per turn of the transcript it
        is asked to distil into project memories.

        The two are separate because they are truncating different things.
        :args-chars is the CALL — a path, a command, a patch header; 160 is
        enough to identify what was done and not enough to paste a file into
        the prompt. :result-chars is the OUTCOME, and it is larger because
        the gotchas worth recording live in error text: a transcript of calls
        without their outcomes cannot say what wasted time, which is most of
        what a reflection is for.

        Raising them buys fidelity and costs context on a prompt that already
        carries every turn of the task. Lowering :result-chars is the one
        that quietly hurts — the reflector stops seeing why a call failed and
        writes vaguer memories, which is the failure mode this whole schema
        exists to avoid."}

 :memory
 {:value
  {:kind-salience {:working 0.3 :episodic 0.45 :procedural 0.5
                   :semantic 0.6 :identity 0.75 :overview 0.95
                   :note 0.5}
   :default-confidence 0.6
   :use-reinforcement 0.05
   :recent-use-bonus 0.15
   :recent-use-window-days 14
   :disuse-decay 0.05
   :decay-floor 0.1
   :salience-cap 0.95
   :effectiveness-weight 0.15
   :effectiveness-cap 0.3
   :confidence-weight 0.25
   :recall-limit 8}
  :kind :policy :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "HOW A MEMORY EARNS ITS PLACE. Adopted from dirge's salience model
        (src/extras/salience.rs), with one deliberate difference: there the
        constants are compiled, and here they are not — the supervisor is the
        role that reads memories and acts on them, so it is the role that
        should be able to retune what counts as worth remembering.

        Effective salience is
          salience
          + recent-use-bonus   (used within recent-use-window-days)
          + effectiveness      (log-damped net of success over failure,
                                weighted and capped)
          + confidence         ((confidence - default) * confidence-weight)

        :kind-salience is the base by KIND, and the ordering is the claim:
        who we are outranks what is true, which outranks how to do things,
        which outranks what happened once, which outranks what we are doing
        right now. :overview sits above all of it — the one orientation note
        per project — and :note is the unclassified default, parked with
        :procedural because an uncategorised memory is almost always a
        how-to.

        SALIENCE AND CONFIDENCE ARE DIFFERENT AXES and collapsing them loses
        the distinction that matters: a fact can be important but contested,
        or trivial but certain. Confidence is weighted low (0.25 over a [0,1]
        range is a ±0.1 swing) so it acts as a tiebreak within a tier rather
        than letting a contested claim jump above a durable one.

        Effectiveness is the axis that makes memory a LOOP rather than a
        list: acting on a memory and reporting whether it worked moves it.
        Log-damped so the first confirmation counts for most of what
        confirmation can buy (+1 ≈ +0.045, +9 ≈ +0.15) and a memory cannot
        be voted to the top by repetition.

        Reinforcement and decay are small on purpose — 0.05 a step means a
        memory needs to be used repeatedly to climb a tier, and needs to be
        ignored for a long time to fall one. A memory system that moved
        faster than the evidence would be a memory system that forgets what
        it learned last week because this week is busy."}

 :prediction-grace-turns
 {:value 4
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How many turns past its window a gate's prediction stays open, waiting
        to see whether the advice was taken LATE.

        A prediction's :window is in turns, and a gate whose advice takes
        longer than its window to follow used to settle :unmet regardless of
        whether it eventually worked — so the tally could not tell a gate
        nobody obeys from one whose advice is sound and slow. Those want
        opposite responses: the first should be reworded or removed, the
        second should have its window widened.

        Within the window a prediction settles :met. Within the grace it
        settles :met-late — the advice worked, later than asked. Past both it
        settles :unmet, which now means what it says: it did not happen.

        Four turns because most of what a gate asks for costs at least one
        turn to attempt and one to verify, and a branch that has not moved on
        it in four is not moving on it."}

 :pause-poll-ms
 {:value 500
  :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How often a paused run checks whether it has been resumed.

        `pause` is applied at a round boundary and the scheduler then waits at
        the top of the next round, so nothing is scheduled while it holds and
        whatever was in flight has already finished — which is exactly what
        interventions/kinds promises a human.

        There is nothing to wait on but a row another process writes, so this
        is a poll. Short enough that a resume feels immediate to the person who
        clicked it; long enough that a run left paused overnight is not
        spinning. The abort flag is read on the same pass, so a paused run
        stays stoppable."}

 :run-health
 {:value
  {:thrash-min-turns 4
   :thrash-mechanics-rate 0.33
   :signals
   {:stage-crashed "STAGE CRASHED — {{detail}}"
    :hollow "NO FILES CHANGED — done was called but the working tree is unchanged; nothing was actually built"
    :nobody-shipped "NO IMPLEMENTOR SHIPPED — the implement round produced nothing verified"
    :tests-failing "TESTS FAILING — {{detail}}"
    :tests-failing-fallback "the tests did not pass"
    :revision-cap "REVISION CAP REACHED — {{revision}} revisions (soft cap {{soft-cap}}) with no verified solution. This is a soft stop for YOU to decide: keep solving with a new approach, or STOP if it is a genuine dead end."
    :thrash "THRASH — a branch spent a third+ of its turns on empty/mis-parsed calls"
    :reviewer-bounced "REVIEWER BOUNCED — the reviewer sent the work back"
    :revising "REVISING — this feature is on revision {{revision}}"
    :heading "## Run health (revision {{revision}})"}}
  :kind :policy :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "WHAT THE SUPERVISOR IS TOLD ABOUT A RUN'S HEALTH, and the two numbers
        behind the one signal that is a judgement rather than a fact.

        Every signal here was a string literal in telemetry.clj. They are the
        supervisor's whole picture of how a run is going — the sentences it
        decides on — and a project working on something other than a
        feature/implement/review loop wants different ones. A sentence in
        compiled code is exactly as unreachable as a threshold in compiled
        code.

        :thrash-min-turns       a branch must have taken this many turns
                                before its mechanics rate means anything. Below
                                it, one malformed call out of two reads as 50%
                                thrash and says nothing.
        :thrash-mechanics-rate  the fraction of a branch's turns spent on
                                empty or mis-parsed calls that counts as
                                thrashing.

        The signals are selmer templates. {{detail}} carries the specific —
        which stage crashed, what the verifier said — and the rest is the
        framing. :tests-failing-fallback is what {{detail}} becomes when the
        verifier left no note, so the sentence still reads as a sentence.

        Not capability-tunable: these are what the supervisor SEES, and a run
        in trouble is the last one that should be shown less of it."}

 :supervisor-digest
 {:value {:per-kind 4 :chars 220}
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How many failure exemplars of each kind the run-health digest shows
        the supervisor, and how much of each failure's text. The digest used
        to carry only RATES — 'this branch thrashed 8 times' — and a rate
        says something is wrong where the exemplar says what: the parse
        error's own complaint, the provider's own words, the failing tool's
        result, each with the journal row id fetch_turn takes. Newest last,
        per kind, because the three kinds are fixed in different places (a
        parse failure in the prompt/format, a provider failure at the
        endpoint, a tool failure in the work itself)."}

 :context-squeeze
 {:value {:factor 0.5 :min-keep-pairs 1 :min-compaction-chars 5000}
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? true
  :doc "How hard a context-overflow squeezes a branch's compaction budget
        (karamazov-d41). When the provider reports the prompt outgrew its
        window — a DETERMINISTIC failure the retry ladder must not touch —
        the loop bumps the branch's squeeze level instead of appending a
        retry message, and render scales :context-budget's :keep-pairs and
        :compaction-chars by :factor per level, floored at the minimums so
        a squeezed branch still sees its current exchange. Observed live on
        the Qwen baseline against llama-server -c 32768: the GLM-sized
        budget assembled ~33k-token prompts and the branch re-sent them on
        backoff, burning wall-clock against a wall."}

 :context-budget
 {:value {:tool-result-chars   4000
          :file-read-chars     60000
          :test-output-lines   25
          :judge-rules-chars   6000
          :judge-transcript-chars 12000
          :grep-ranges         20
          :inbox-lines         3
          :tree-lines          8
          :keep-pairs          10
          :compaction-chars    50000
          :diff-chars          12000
          :shell-output-chars  8000
          :shared-code-chars   700
          :ledger-claim-chars  180
          :critic-claim-chars  400
          ;; The base output budget a truncation RETRY doubles from when the
          ;; provider config sets no :max-tokens of its own — without it the
          ;; "retry at a doubled budget" contract silently degraded to a
          ;; same-budget repeat, reproducing the truncation it exists to
          ;; escape (blt.38).
          :retry-base-tokens   8192}
  :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "HOW MUCH THE MODEL GETS TO SEE. Every one of these was a constant in
        code, and together they are the most project-specific numbers in the
        harness: a codebase of 200-line namespaces read by a 200k-context model
        wants nothing like the same values as one with generated files read by a
        small local model.

        They are also the numbers most likely to be the real cause of a run
        going wrong, and the hardest to diagnose from the outside — a branch
        that cannot see the line its test failed on looks exactly like a branch
        that cannot read a stack trace.

        :tool-result-chars      one tool result, before it is truncated
        :file-read-chars        one `read` of a file
        :test-output-lines      the TAIL of a failing test run a branch is
                                shown; the tail rather than the head because a
                                failure names itself at the end
        :judge-rules-chars      the harness's own rules, as the judge sees them
        :judge-transcript-chars the transcript under judgement
        :tree-lines             files the shared-tree block names — the paths
                                OTHER branches on this run have changed. Team
                                workers share one working tree on purpose (the
                                parts of a feature belong in the same files),
                                and the mailbox only ever carried what a
                                sibling chose to announce. One line per file
                                rather than per write, so eight is a wide tree
                                rather than eight edits.
        :grep-ranges            distinct line ranges a search reports
        :inbox-lines            unread messages previewed in the context block
        :keep-pairs             recent exchanges kept VERBATIM before
                                compaction unloads them (LR-4)
        :compaction-chars       total content before compaction engages at all
        :diff-chars             the working-tree diff a branch and the judge
                                are shown
        :shell-output-chars     one shell command's output. Separate from
                                :tool-result-chars, and larger, because a
                                build log's useful part is at the end of a
                                great deal of noise
        :shared-code-chars      how much of a sibling's artifact CODE the
                                shared block carries. A theorem STATEMENT is
                                what a sibling needs in order to build on a
                                lemma; the proof body is not
        :ledger-claim-chars     how much of a claim the settled-state ledger
                                shows. gen-18 averaged ~340 characters per
                                claim and its 79 confirmed artifacts render to
                                ~6,800 tokens unabridged; the ledger's job is
                                to let a branch see WHAT is settled, and the
                                exact statement is one `fetch_artifact` away
        :critic-claim-chars     how much of a claim the critic is shown per
                                artifact when scoring a branch

        The ANSWER under judgement is deliberately absent from this list: it is
        the thing being judged, and truncating it could hide the very flaw the
        judge is looking for. A key here that nothing reads would be worse than
        no key at all — `:run :loop` was documented, parsed from an env var and
        read by no live code for a whole phase — so a test asserts every key in
        this map is referenced somewhere in src.

        A :cost-ceiling, so the capability tier may not raise them: every one
        trades context for evidence, and a struggling run is the last one that
        should be handed more context on the harness's own initiative. A
        PROJECT raising them deliberately is a different act."}

 :critic-cumulative-objectives
 {:value [:progress]
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The objectives that ACCUMULATE over a branch's life, and which a cull
        decision therefore must not compare on.

        A cumulative score rises with age and nothing else, so comparing on it
        lets any mature branch dominate any young one indefinitely — the age
        bias the juvenile grace period only postpones. It is also the wrong
        question: what a branch already confirmed is in the log and cannot be
        lost by culling it, so survival turns on where the line is GOING and
        on what it uniquely covers.

        Derived, not listed: the survival set is :critic-objectives minus
        this. It was a second hardcoded vector, so retuning the rubric left
        the cull reading the old one."}

 :critic-scale
 {:value {:min 1 :max 5}
  :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The range a critic score may take. Read by BOTH halves of the loop —
        prompts/critic.md asks for it and critic.clj's parser accepts it —
        so widening the rubric cannot leave the parser behind. It was a
        `[1-5]` literal inside the parser's regex while the objective NAMES
        were already data here, which meant :critic-objectives was
        advertised as retunable and was not: adding a fifth objective left
        the pattern matching only the original four, and parse-scores fails
        CLOSED, so every scoring silently returned nil and the retention
        cascade fell through to its no-scores rule."}

 :critic-objectives
 {:value [:progress :momentum :distinctness :viability] :kind :threshold
  :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "The critic's scoring rubric — all maximize, 1..5. :progress is
        engine-confirmed work accumulated over the branch's life,
        :momentum is whether the recent turns are productive, :distinctness
        is difference from the sibling theses (the beam's diversity lives
        here), :viability is whether the line has anywhere left to go.
        Retention reads all but :progress (critic.clj
        survival-objectives); :fork-invite-floor keys on these same names.
        The score prompt still names these four in prose until it moves to
        prompts/ (tier 2a) — until then this list tunes what parse-scores
        ACCEPTS, not what the prompt asks for."}

 :decompose-max-depth
 {:value 3 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "How deep the decompose recursion may go before a stuck unit is a
        hard failure rather than split again. A cost ceiling: each level
        multiplies sub-agents, and a unit that still won't pass its tests
        three levels down is not a size problem."}

 :decompose-max-parts
 {:value 4 :kind :cost-ceiling :capability-tunable? false
  :doc "Most sub-units the architect may split one unit into."}

 :judge-rules
 ;; drg-4026 #55: the judge's deterministic finalization rules — what counts
 ;; as a test run, a code edit, an outside claim — as data. A fixed
 ;; test-runner ecosystem is project policy, not kernel code: a Python or
 ;; Rust project retunes :test-run-regex and :verifier-message without
 ;; touching src. Regexes are stored as strings (EDN has no regex literal);
 ;; re-pattern at read.
 {:value
  {;; What the judge's REPLY is expected to look like. The prompt asking for
   ;; this shape is prompts/judge.md, which is userspace — so while these
   ;; lived in judge.clj a supervisor could reword the request and silently
   ;; break the parser reading the answer, with no error anywhere: an
   ;; unmatched verdict falls through to :complete, which fails OPEN. The
   ;; two halves are one decision and belong in one editable place.
   :verdict-line-regex "(?i)\\bVERDICT\\b"
   :findings-regex "(?is)FINDINGS:\\s*(.+)$"
   ;; Ordered, first match wins, and each entry is a verdict with the words
   ;; that must ALL appear on the verdict line for it to apply. The order IS
   ;; the precedence rule: negative before abstain before positive, because
   ;; INCOMPLETE contains COMPLETE and "NOT COMPLETE" negates it. A plain
   ;; word-set per verdict could not express either.
   :verdict-rules [[:incomplete ["INCOMPLETE"]]
                   [:incomplete ["NOT" "COMPLETE"]]
                   [:abstain ["ABSTAIN"]]
                   [:complete ["COMPLETE"]]]
   ;; What an empty or tokenless reply means. FAIL-OPEN on purpose: a judge
   ;; that cannot answer must never be able to wedge the loop.
   :verdict-default :complete

   :test-run-regex
   "(?i)-M:test|-A:test|run-tests|\\bjolt\\s+test\\b|\\bpytest\\b|\\bcargo\\s+test\\b|\\bnpm\\s+test\\b"
   :code-ext-regex "(?i)\\.(clj|cljc|cljs|edn|py|ts|js|rs|go)$"
   :claim-regex "(?i)\\b(tested|all tests pass|passing|verified|ran the (tests|suite))\\b"
   :outside-claim-regex "(?i)(https?://|www\\.|according to|the docs say|the spec says|the api returns)"
   ;; #56: matched against the REGISTERED tool names (and the run's rows).
   ;; fetch_artifact / fetch_turn contain no web-family words, so they never
   ;; count as reaching out; a project that registers a web tool disables
   ;; the block by existing — its runs COULD have checked the claim.
   :outside-reach-regex "(?i)(web|browse|curl|wget|http)"
   :verifier-message
   "You changed code but never ran a test. Run the suite (`jolt -M:test`) or the focused test, confirm it passes, then finish."
   :claim-message
   "Your answer claims the work was tested or verified, but the run shows no test was run. Run it and confirm, or drop the claim."
   :outside-message
   "Your answer attributes a claim to something outside the repo (a URL or a secondary source). samizdat's registered tools work on the local repo; nothing in this run could have checked that source. Cite evidence from the repo, or from your own verified work in it."}
   :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
   :doc "Deterministic finalization rules for the judge's free blocks. The
         detection engine lives in judge.clj; the vocabulary it matches on
         lives here, so it retunes per project at runtime."}

  :focused-verify
  ;; drg-4026 #47/48: the focused-verify conventions — which changed paths
  ;; count as test files, how a test path maps to its namespace, and the
  ;; command that runs just those namespaces. Project conventions, not
  ;; kernel code: a pytest or maven project retunes these without a
  ;; rebuild. The derivations live in verify.clj and read these at fire
  ;; time. :cmd-prefix is concatenated with the derived expression and a
  ;; closing single quote for sh -c; the expression's namespaces come
  ;; through :ns-whitelist-regex, so the quoted payload genuinely contains
  ;; no single quotes (provenance R3-1).
  {:value
   {:test-file-regex "(?i)(^|/)(test|spec)s?/|[_-](test|spec)\\.[a-z]+$"
    :root-strip-regex "^(test|gui|src)/"
    :ext-strip-regex "\\.cljc?$"
    :ns-whitelist-regex "[a-zA-Z0-9.*-]+"
    :cmd-prefix "jolt -A:test -e '"}
   :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
   :doc "The focused-verify conventions as data (see the comment above)."}

  :ship-gates
  ;; drg-4026 #44: the model-free ship rungs as data, compiled at load by
  ;; ship.clj into predicate/message closures fired on the computed
  ;; evidence (answer, problem, evidence, uncovered-numbers — the symbols
  ;; the forms below reference). Vector order is the cond order it
  ;; replaces. The number-coverage rung guards against a fabricated
  ;; verification report but only fires when the run produced evidence to
  ;; check against: a coding run's proof is its test run, not confirmed
  ;; claims, so empty evidence must not refuse every figure (the first
  ;; self-modification run did the work correctly and could not ship it).
  {:value
   [{:name :answer-exists
     :when (str/blank? (str answer))
     ;; Shows the CALL, not just the missing name. base/missing already works
     ;; this way, on the reasoning that a bare list of argument names teaches
     ;; nothing — gen-20 B1 called proof_start without its arguments five
     ;; times, three producing the byte-identical message, and was culled for
     ;; it. This rung was the last place still saying only the name, and it
     ;; cost two live runs: both did the work, passed their tests, closed
     ;; their task, then spent their remaining turns calling `done` empty and
     ;; ended :exhausted with nothing shipped.
     :message-form (str "`done` needs an `answer` — the text a person reads as"
                        " the result of this run. Not a status line: say what"
                        " you built and what shows it works.\n\n"
                        "```tool-call\n"
                        "{\"name\": \"done\", \"args\": {\"answer\":"
                        " \"<what you built, and the evidence for it>\"}}\n"
                        "```")}
    {:name :figure-coverage
     :when (and (seq evidence) (seq uncovered-numbers))
     :message-form (str "Your answer states figures no artifact supports: "
                        (str/join ", " (map (fn [x] (str "`" x "`"))
                                            (take 8 uncovered-numbers)))
                        ".\nA number in an answer has to come from something"
                        " confirmed or measured — that is the difference between a"
                        " report and a fabricated one. Either verify these or"
                        " remove them from the answer.")}
    {:name :engages-problem
     :when (and (not (str/blank? (str problem)))
                (not (engages-problem? problem answer)))
     :message-form (str "This answer shares no substantive term with the problem"
                        " statement. Whatever it establishes, it is not an answer to"
                        " the question that was asked.")}
    ;; karamazov-g86, dirge completeness_gate.rs: the silent-exhaustion tell.
    ;; One sentence in the model's own voice planning more work means the run
    ;; is stopping halfway and saying so. The detection conjunction and its
    ;; do-not-widen warning live with the wordlists
    ;; (:completeness-* in wordlists.edn); this rung is just the decision.
    {:name :completeness
     :when (unfinished-claim? answer)
     :message-form (str "One sentence of this answer says, in your own voice,"
                        " that work remains — a first-person plan to do more"
                        " is not a result. Either do that work now and finish"
                        " again, or restate the answer as what IS done and put"
                        " what is left on the board (the task tool), so the"
                        " record is honest either way.")}]
   :kind :threshold :capability-tunable? false
   :doc "The lexical ship rungs (drg-4026 #44) — adding a rung is a data
         edit; the evidence computation stays in ship.clj."}

  :tool-vocab
  ;; Tier 1a: the tool vocabularies the gates and their settlers read — data
 ;; here, alongside the thresholds, so a vocabulary changes at runtime
 ;; without a rebuild. agent-test walks every name against the registered
 ;; run-tools (provenance R3-6): a name the loop cannot dispatch is a gate whose
 ;; prediction silently narrows to :unmet.
 {:verification #{"eval" "shell"}
  :shipping #{"write_file" "edit_file" "shell" "reload_cells" "manifest"
              "done" "give_up" "thesis" "branch_theses"}
  :file-write #{"write_file" "edit_file"}
  ;; The storm guard's tool classification (karamazov-ekk). :storm-exempt is
  ;; the read-only surface — repeated identical reads are over-studying's
  ;; business, not a storm — plus done/give_up, whose repetition belongs to
  ;; the done-blocked and last-call rungs: storm withholding a done that
  ;; last-call is simultaneously forcing would be one guard punishing what
  ;; another demands (dirge hit that conflict three separate times).
  ;; :storm-mutating tools clear the window's non-mutating entries when they
  ;; fire, and still count amongst themselves. Everything in neither set is
  ;; counted normally.
  :storm-exempt #{"read_file" "grep" "doc" "manual" "introspect" "cells"
                  "recall" "fetch_artifact" "fetch_turn" "branch_theses"
                  "lsp" "skill" "policy" "complete" "done" "give_up"}
  :storm-mutating #{"write_file" "edit_file" "shell" "eval" "reload_cells"
                    "cell" "prompt" "manifest" "experiment" "remember"
                    "forget"}
  :settle-called
  {:milestone        #{"done"}
   :branch-out       #{"branch_theses"}
   :repopulate       #{"branch_theses"}
   :emergency-review #{"done" "thesis"}
   :stuck            #{"thesis"}
   :storm            #{"done" "give_up"}
   :safe-state       #{"thesis" "done" "give_up"}
   :done-blocked     #{"done" "give_up"}
   :turn-budget      #{"done" "give_up"}
   :wind-down        #{"done"}
   :last-call        #{"done" "give_up"}
   :reflection       #{"introspect" "reload_cells" "cells"}
   :studying         #{"write_file" "edit_file" "shell" "reload_cells"
                       "done" "give_up"}}}

 :gates
 ;; Tier 3a: the steer policy as data — pilot gates first. A :gates entry
 ;; carries :when as an EDN FORM, compiled by gates.clj at load into the
 ;; closure shape the arbiter reads (the manifest :dispatches are the
 ;; precedent: EDN predicates evaluated at compile time). The form sees the
 ;; gate context keys (directive done-block branch max-turns branch-count
 ;; safe-state-coverage) and calls the same accessors the hand-written
 ;; closures did — (threshold k) reads the config atom at fire time, so
 ;; tuning a threshold stays runtime-editable; only the form structure
 ;; compiles at load. :message-file names a prompts/ file; {{turn-count}}
 ;; and {{max-turns}} interpolate at fire time.
 [{:gate :reflection
   :priority 13
   :budget :max-reflection-nudges
   :doc "A quiet-turn nudge to reflect on the loop itself. Lowest priority, so
         it is passed over whenever any real steer holds and surfaces only on a
         turn where nothing else does. The loop is a mycelium workflow the
         branch can see (introspect) and reshape (reload_cells); a run that
         never reconsiders its own machinery keeps whatever friction it started
         with. Fires on a cadence, not a condition, which is why it sits below
         every gate that fires because something is wrong."
   :when (and (state/active? branch)
              ;; The branch's OWN turns: a fork reflects on a fresh cadence
              ;; rather than inheriting its parent's position (blt.16).
              (pos? (state/own-turn-count branch))
              (zero? (mod (state/own-turn-count branch)
                          (threshold :reflection-cadence))))
   :message-file "reflection"
   :prediction "the branch inspects or reshapes its loop"
   :window 1}

  {:gate :prologue-cap
   :priority 9
   :budget nil
   :doc "The branch has produced nothing at all. Every other guard is
         principled-blind here: the stall counter arms on a progress event, the
         stuck gate needs failures, and a branch making successful, varied,
         useless calls trips neither.

         Build phase only (vf-9wx). A banked sketch is deliberately :neutral
         with progress? false — a plan is not progress — so a branch dropped
         back into :explore by a reframe accrues nothing while it re-plans, and
         without this guard is told at turn 41 that it is 41 turns in with
         nothing verified. True, and useless to a branch doing exactly what the
         harness just asked of it."
   :when (and (= :build (:phase branch))
              (not (:any-progress? branch))
              ;; OWN turns: what THIS branch has produced nothing in (blt.16).
              (>= (state/own-turn-count branch) (threshold :prologue-cap)))
   :message-file "prologue-cap"
   :message-suffix "\n\nYou are {{turn-count}} turns in with nothing verified."
   :prediction "the branch produces its first artifact"
   :window 3}

  ;; Tier 3b tranche 1: the seven gates whose messages are a prompts/ file
  ;; (plus {{turn-count}}/{{max-turns}} interpolation) — no message-builder
  ;; machinery needed. The five that build messages from branch state
  ;; (done-blocked, human-directive, safe-state, stuck, studying,
  ;; progress-stalled) remain closures until :message compiles as a form.

  ;; Priority 2.5: below safe-state (a branch just told it cannot ship is
  ;; not forced to ship) and above wind-down, so in the final turns the hard
  ;; force wins over the soft nudge. Fractional to slot between the two
  ;; without renumbering the ladder; priorities need only be distinct
  ;; (gate-config-is-coherent).
  {:gate :last-call
   :priority 2.5
   :budget :max-last-call-steers
   :tool "done"
   :doc "The branch is in its last turns and still has not shipped. wind-down
         already asked it to; this FORCES the attempt — a done-tool prefill, so
         the model must continue inside a done call rather than exploring or
         rambling for its final turns. A live team run (karamazov-pab) had every
         worker exhaust its cap with no done or give_up; a forced partial that
         the reviewer/critic can judge beats a silent exhaustion the supervisor
         has to re-task blind. If the branch truly has nothing, the done-gate
         refuses it and the done-blocked rung takes over toward giving up."
   :when (and (state/active? branch)
              max-turns
              (>= (state/turn-count branch)
                  (- max-turns (threshold :last-call-window))))
   :message-file "last-call"
   :message-suffix "\n\nYou are at turn {{turn-count}} of {{max-turns}} — your last turns. Ship your best partial now with done."
   :prediction "the branch ships"
   :window 2}

  {:gate :storm
   :priority 2.7
   :budget :max-storm-forces
   :tool "give_up"
   :doc "The storm guard's escalation rung (karamazov-ekk). The withhold in
         phases.edn blocks each identical repeat as it is attempted; this
         rung answers the model that keeps attempting it anyway — several
         consecutive calls withheld means the branch is spending its turns
         re-asking for a result it already has, and forcing an honest
         give_up (a give_up-tool prefill, not prose) hands the task back
         with an account of what was tried, where a silent grind hands back
         nothing. Same forced-tool mechanism as last-call, for the same
         karamazov-pab reason."
   :when (and (state/active? branch)
              (>= (or (:storm-strikes branch) 0)
                  (threshold :storm-strikes-to-force)))
   :message-file "storm-force"
   :prediction "the branch abandons the repeated call honestly — give_up with
                an account of what was tried, or a genuinely different
                approach"
   :window 2}

  {:gate :wind-down
   :priority 3
   :budget :max-wind-down-steers
   :doc "The branch has passed the fraction of the turn cap reserved for
         shipping. UCLA's harness reserves a deadline tail for assembly and
         shipping, and a run with no such tail spends its whole budget
         exploring and ships nothing. This steer outranks every nudge rung so
         a branch near its cap is told to ship before it is nagged to do more,
         but it never outranks done-blocked or safe-state: a branch that has
         just been told it cannot ship is not being asked to ship."
   :when (and (state/active? branch)
              (>= (state/turn-count branch)
                  (* (threshold :wind-down-fraction) (max 1 max-turns))))
   :message-file "wind-down"
   :message-suffix "\n\nYou are at turn {{turn-count}} of {{max-turns}}."
   :prediction "the branch ships"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :emergency-review
   :priority 4
   :budget :max-emergency-reviews
   :doc "At the cull threshold but holding a recent confirmation. Rather than
         culling the branch that produced the most, tell it to ship what it
         has or change approach. Guarded since a live run re-fired it on
         three consecutive boundaries: the precondition persists while the
         branch is busy complying, which is what re-fire guards are for."
   :when (and (>= (:consecutive-failures branch) (threshold :cull-threshold))
              (state/confirmed-in-last branch (threshold :cull-recent-window)))
   :message-file "emergency-review"
   :prediction "the branch ships or changes technique"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :milestone
   :priority 5
   :budget :max-milestone-nudges
   :doc "First confirmed artifact on this branch that engages what the branch
         is working on. Runs that do not ship at this moment usually fail:
         after a confirmation the instinct is to push for more, and that
         usually loses the verified result. Relevance-filtered because the
         gate congratulated a branch for verifying that between/3 works
         (vf-8fl), which is the same failure the zebra run produced."
   :when (state/has-relevant-confirmed? branch)
   :message-file "milestone"
   :prediction "the branch ships within two turns"
   :window 2}

  ;; Ranked just above branch-out. Both ask for offspring, but this one
  ;; fires because the beam is dying rather than because a branch is
  ;; thriving, and refilling an empty slot is the more urgent of the two.
  ;; The scheduler marks the branch (it is the only thing that knows the
  ;; alive count, the target, and who is strongest); this rung is what
  ;; actually asks.
  {:gate :repopulate
   :priority 6
   :budget :max-repopulates
   :tool "branch_theses"
   :doc "The beam has fallen below its target width and this branch is the
         strongest survivor. It was an invitation appended straight from the
         scheduler until it became this: that carried no prediction, settled
         nothing, and showed up in no gate tally (gen-17 sent 12 in its
         first 120 turns and 9 were declined), and was a second harness
         voice on a boundary that had already had its one steer."
   :when (when-let [due (:repopulate-due branch)]
           ;; Only for a mark the branch has not already been asked about,
           ;; so a mark that survives on the branch does not re-fire every
           ;; boundary while it is busy complying.
           (let [last-fired (->> (:gate-history branch)
                                 (filter #(= :repopulate (:gate %)))
                                 (map :turn)
                                 (reduce max -1000))]
             (> due last-fired)))
   :message-file "repopulate"
   :prediction "the branch calls branch_theses"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :branch-out
   :priority 7
   :budget :max-branch-outs
   :tool "branch_theses"
   :doc "A branch that just confirmed something, with room left in the beam.

         This is the reproduction half of the loop. The beam had selection
         (critic scores, Pareto retention) and no variation: branch_theses
         existed, the scheduler invited forks, and across every live run
         the count of forked children was zero — seven invitations went out
         in one run and every one was declined. An invitation the model may
         decline is not a mechanism; a gate with a settled prediction is.

         Fires on evidence rather than on hope: a confirmation is the
         fitness signal, so the branch worth reproducing from is the one
         that just proved something ABOUT THE PROBLEM — a fork costs another
         engine process and another model call per turn, and is not worth
         spending on a branch that confirmed its own tooling. Silent once the
         run is winding down, because a new line that late cannot finish."
   :when (let [last-fired (->> (:gate-history branch)
                               (filter #(= :branch-out (:gate %)))
                               (map :turn)
                               (reduce max -1000))]
           ;; Not while the branch is still acting on the last ask.
           (and (state/has-relevant-confirmed? branch)
                (< (or branch-count 0) (threshold :max-total-branches))
                (>= (- (state/turn-count branch) last-fired)
                    (threshold :branch-out-cooldown))
                (< (state/turn-count branch)
                   (* (threshold :wind-down-fraction)
                      (max 1 (or max-turns 40))))))
   :message-file "branch-out"
   :prediction "the branch calls branch_theses"
   :window 3}

  ;; Reads the branch's own record of which fractions it has been told
  ;; about. Taking it from the context instead meant the caller had to
  ;; remember to thread it back, and on the first live run it did not — the
  ;; gate re-fired on every turn past 60%.
  {:gate :turn-budget
   :priority 12
   :budget nil
   ;; drg-4026 #4: the effect this gate has on branch state, applied by
   ;; loop/apply-effects — the fraction notices it has delivered are folded
   ;; into the branch so the gate stops re-firing (the comment above the
   ;; entry is the story of forgetting to do exactly that).
   :effect :notified-fractions
   :doc "The turn cap was enforced but invisible to the model, so it could not
         budget against it (dirge PR 738)."
   :when (let [used (/ (double (state/turn-count branch)) (max 1 max-turns))
               notified (:notified-fractions branch #{})]
           (boolean (some #(and (>= used %) (not (contains? notified %)))
                          (threshold :turn-budget-notices))))
   :message-suffix "**Turn budget**: you have used {{turn-count}} of {{max-turns}} turns. Land what you can verify rather than opening a new line of attack."
   ;; Says what it MEASURES. The rule reads :settle-called :turn-budget,
   ;; which is #{"done" "give_up"} — terminating, not converging. A branch
   ;; that edits and writes its way toward finishing was settling `unmet`
   ;; against a prediction it had no way to satisfy short of stopping, and
   ;; the met-rate is the only evidence any of this steering works.
   :prediction "the branch ships or gives up"
   :window 3}

  ;; Tier 3b tranche 2: the six message-builder gates. Their :message-form is
  ;; an EDN form compiled like :when — same gate-context keys, plus
  ;; prompt/threshold/state — evaluated at fire time.

  {:gate :human-directive
   :priority 0
   :budget nil
   :doc "A human told the branch to do something. Outranks every machine gate,
         which is dirge PR 717's finding arriving as a design property rather
         than a bug fix."
   :when (some? directive)
   ;; :payload-text is attached by the drains — the payload column is a JSON
   ;; blob ({"text": "..."}), and rendering it raw framed `{"text":"[watch]
   ;; ..."}` as the human's words (blt.38).
   :message-form (str "**A human has intervened in this run.**\n\n"
                      (or (:payload-text directive) (:payload directive))
                      "\n\nThis takes precedence over anything the harness has told"
                      " you. Act on it on this turn.")
   :prediction "the branch acts on the directive on the next turn"
   :window 1}

  {:gate :done-blocked
   :priority 1
   :budget :max-done-blocks
   :doc "done was called without the evidence it requires. The message says
         which gate is unmet, because 'not yet' with no reason produces
         another identical attempt."
   :when (some? done-block)
   :message-form done-block
   :prediction "the branch supplies the missing evidence or gives up"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :safe-state
   :priority 2
   :budget :max-safe-state-aborts
   :doc "DS1's third failure rung. The branch has failed repeatedly since a
         green ship-verify, so what it changed since is the suspect, not
         the approach. Advisory by default: it names the fallback rather
         than performing it, because a restore that is not fully covered
         produces a session that never existed."
   :when (state/safe-state-due? branch (threshold :cull-threshold)
                               (threshold :safe-state-multiple))
   :message-form (str (prompt "safe-state")
                      "\n\nYour last green test run was at turn "
                      (:green-snapshot branch) ". "
                      (if (:ok safe-state-coverage)
                        (str "Every turn since is journalled, so replaying up to"
                             " that point is a state the branch actually occupied"
                             " — you can retrace it by hand turn by turn.")
                        (str "The turn log no longer reaches that point: "
                             (:reason safe-state-coverage)
                             " Start from what the journal still shows, or ship"
                             " what you have.")))
   :prediction "the branch changes technique, or ships what it has"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :stuck
   :priority 8
   :budget :max-stuck-hints
   ;; drg-4026 #4: the effect this gate has on branch state, applied by
   ;; loop/apply-effects — the reframe that withholds the failing claim.
   :effect :begin-reframe
   :doc "Consecutive failed or repetitive verifications. Keyed on failure,
         which is why the progress gate below exists as well.

         The one gate that changes branch state rather than only speaking.
         It fired at cull-threshold originally, so the advice to change
         course arrived on the turn the branch became killable for not
         having changed it (vf-31m); stuck-threshold is now strictly lower
         and the firing opens a grace window. And it WITHHOLDS (vf-49o):
         the loop answers a firing by calling state/begin-reframe, after
         which re-verifying the failing approach is refused on every engine
         until the branch puts a different one on record. A gate is data
         and cannot mutate the branch, so that effect lives in the loop
         beside the turn-budget bookkeeping."
   :when (>= (:consecutive-failures branch) (threshold :stuck-threshold))
   :message-form (str (prompt "stuck")
                      (when-let [c (:last-failed-claim branch)]
                        (str "\n\n**Withheld**, until you put a different approach"
                             " on record:\n\n> " c "\n\nThat claim will not reach an"
                             " engine — any engine — while this stands. Everything"
                             " else is open, including a smaller piece of the same"
                             " goal. The next " (threshold :reframe-grace) " turns are"
                             " yours to change course in; you will not be culled for"
                             " the failures that led here."))
                      ;; The reflexion log: everything this branch has already
                      ;; tried and abandoned (the newest is the withheld claim
                      ;; above), so it diverges instead of circling back to a
                      ;; dead end it has forgotten.
                      (when-let [earlier (seq (butlast (:abandoned branch)))]
                        (str "\n\nApproaches you have already tried and abandoned"
                             " — do not retry these:\n"
                             (str/join "\n" (map #(str "- " %) earlier)))))
   :prediction "the branch retracts, decomposes, or changes technique"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :file-thrash
   :priority 9.5
   :budget :max-file-thrash-nudges
   :doc "The same-file streak (karamazov-g86, dirge context_depth.rs):
         consecutive calls all touching the same file(s) is grinding, not
         iterating — the branch has narrowed to one spot and lost the task.
         Between prologue-cap and progress-stalled: sharper than the generic
         stall (it names the files), broader than the build-phase prologue.
         Settles by rule, not by tool: met when the streak actually broke."
   :when (>= (get-in branch [:file-touch :streak] 0)
             (threshold :file-thrash-threshold))
   :message-form (str (prompt "file-thrash")
                      (when-let [fs (seq (get-in branch [:file-touch :files]))]
                        (str "\n\nThe files in the streak: "
                             (str/join ", " (sort fs)))))
   :prediction "the branch touches a different file, steps back to a no-file
                action, or closes the task"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :progress-stalled
   :priority 10
   :budget :max-stall-nudges
   :doc "Turns passing with no progress event, after the branch has shown it
         can make progress. Arms only after the first, so exploration is never
         nudged."
   :when (and (:any-progress? branch)
              (>= (:turns-since-progress branch)
                  (threshold :progress-stall-threshold)))
   :message-form (str (prompt "progress-stalled")
                      "\n\nNothing has advanced in " (:turns-since-progress branch)
                      " turns.")
   :prediction "the branch produces a new confirmed artifact or discharges a sub-claim"
   :window 3}

  {:gate :studying
   :priority 11
   :budget :max-studying-nudges
   :doc "The supervisor's stall: the branch shipped something, then lapsed into
         inspecting — read/grep/eval turn after turn, all succeeding, changing
         nothing. The failure gates key on errors (there are none) and the
         progress gate keys on confirmed artifacts (a coding run makes none),
         so this is the case both are blind to. It watches the branch's own
         turn pattern instead — see samizdat.agent.supervisor, which is also
         what a multi-agent supervisor sub-loop reads. Coding-tunable, since it
         reads tool mechanics, not verification."
   :when (and (state/active? branch)
              (supervisor/over-studying? (tool-vocab :shipping)
                                         (:turns branch)
                                         (threshold :studying-turns)))
   :message-form (supervisor/stall-nudge (tool-vocab :shipping)
                                         (:turns branch)
                                         (threshold :studying-turns))
   :prediction "the branch commits a change, runs a test, or ships"
   :window 3}]

 :forceable-tools
 ;; Schemas for the tools a gate may FORCE via native tool_choice (tier 3b:
 ;; data). Only the terminal tools — forcing a mid-task tool would be the
 ;; harness deciding rather than steering. Minimal: enough for the provider
 ;; to accept the function.
 {"done" {:name "done"
          :description "Finish the task and return the final answer."
          :parameters {:type "object"
                       :properties {:answer {:type "string"
                                             :description "The final answer, or the best partial result so far."}}
                       :required ["answer"]}}
  "give_up" {:name "give_up"
             :description "Abandon the task, stating why it cannot be finished."
             :parameters {:type "object"
                          :properties {:reason {:type "string"}}
                          :required ["reason"]}}}}
